Do not process the input in parts at each "-" operand.
Without -i all the files form one stream, but a "-" operand made
sed_main() call process_files() on the files up to and including it
right away, and then again for the rest. Each part was processed as if
it were all the input: line numbers started again, "$" matched the last
line of the part, and q ended only the part, after which the next part
went on reading what the first left unread, minus one line that had
already been read ahead and was lost. "seq 4 | sed 1q -" printed 1, 3
and 4 instead of 1. Only mark the operand as standard input, and leave
all the processing to the single process_files() call at the end.

---
 editors/sed.c |    4 +---
 1 file changed, 1 insertion(+), 3 deletions(-)

--- a/editors/sed.c
+++ b/editors/sed.c
@@ -1766,10 +1766,8 @@
 			G.last_input_file++;
  start:
 			if (!(opt & OPT_in_place)) {
-				if (LONE_DASH(*argv)) {
+				if (LONE_DASH(*argv))
 					*argv = (char*)bb_msg_standard_input;
-					process_files();
-				}
 				continue;
 			}
 
